  "image_name": "1cf19ef6eda0e551037e864948f5bc39.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a lymph node develops and how specialized lymphatic endothelial cells are patterned. On the left, an early “lymph node anlage” forms during embryonic stages as green LTi cells gather around a vascular and lymphatic meshwork near mesenchymal cells and blood vessels. The central schematic shows a more organized early lymph node, with afferent and efferent lymphatics connected to a capsule-like structure surrounding the node. Distinct endothelial layers are indicated: “ceiling” lymphatic endothelial cells around the upper sinus region and “floor” endothelial cells lining the lower side, alongside mesenchymal and smooth muscle cells. Immune cells, including LTi cells and dendritic cells, are shown entering and accumulating within this developing structure.\n\nThe right panel highlights a signaling mechanism that appears to establish floor LEC identity. RANKL from a marginal reticular cell acts through RANK on the endothelial layer, increasing molecules such as MADCAM1, α2β integrin, and CCL20 while reducing ACKR4. CSF1 is also shown as a candidate signal, possibly acting through CSF1R, linked to nearby CD11b+ myeloid precursors and subcapsular sinus macrophages. Overall, the figure emphasizes staged lymph node assembly and local niche signals that shape specialized lymphatic endothelium and associated immune cell populations.",
